### What is flatMap?

`flatMap` is a stream operation that does two things at once. For each element in the stream it applies a function that returns a new stream, and then it concatenates all those returned streams into a single stream. The result is that one level of nesting disappears.

When your data is a list of departments and each department holds a list of employees, the structure is nested. `flatMap` lets you walk every employee across every department as if they were one flat sequence.

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class FlattenDemo {
    public static List<Employee> allEmployees(List<Department> departments) {
        return departments.stream()
                .flatMap(d -> d.getEmployees().stream())
                .collect(Collectors.toList());
    }
}
```

Each `d.getEmployees().stream()` is a small stream of one department's employees, and `flatMap` joins them into one big `Stream<Employee>`.

### How do you model the nested Department and Employee?

The department holds a list of employees. Salary uses `BigDecimal` because it is money.

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

public class Employee {
    private final String name;
    private final BigDecimal salary;

    public Employee(String name, BigDecimal salary) {
        this.name = name;
        this.salary = salary;
    }

    public String getName() { return name; }
    public BigDecimal getSalary() { return salary; }

    @Override
    public String toString() { return name; }
}

class Department {
    private final String name;
    private final List<Employee> employees;

    public Department(String name, List<Employee> employees) {
        this.name = name;
        this.employees = employees;
    }

    public String getName() { return name; }
    public List<Employee> getEmployees() { return employees; }
}
```

### How do you run the flattening end to end?

Here is a complete program that builds two departments and flattens their employees into one list.

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Department engineering = new Department("Engineering", List.of(
                new Employee("Rajesh", new BigDecimal("120000")),
                new Employee("Anita", new BigDecimal("115000"))
        ));
        Department hr = new Department("HR", List.of(
                new Employee("Priya", new BigDecimal("95000"))
        ));

        List<Department> departments = List.of(engineering, hr);

        List<Employee> flat = departments.stream()
                .flatMap(d -> d.getEmployees().stream())
                .collect(Collectors.toList());

        System.out.println(flat);
        System.out.println("total = " + flat.size());
    }
}
```

```text
[Rajesh, Anita, Priya]
total = 3
```

### Why can map not do this?

`map` is a one-to-one transform. Each element goes in, exactly one element comes out. If you map each department to a stream of its employees, each department produces one stream, so you get a stream of streams. The nesting stays.

```java
// WRONG: map produces Stream<Stream<Employee>>, still nested
Stream<Stream<Employee>> nested = departments.stream()
        .map(d -> d.getEmployees().stream());
// You cannot collect this into a flat List<Employee> directly.
```

```java
// CORRECT: flatMap merges the inner streams into one Stream<Employee>
List<Employee> flat = departments.stream()
        .flatMap(d -> d.getEmployees().stream())
        .collect(Collectors.toList());
```

The wrong version compiles but leaves you holding a `Stream<Stream<Employee>>`, which you would have to flatten yourself. `flatMap` does that flattening for you.

### How do map and flatMap compare?

| Aspect | map | flatMap |
|--------|-----|---------|
| Cardinality | one-to-one | one-to-many |
| Mapper returns | a value `R` | a `Stream<R>` |
| Input `Stream<Department>` | `Stream<Stream<Employee>>` | `Stream<Employee>` |
| Flattening | no | yes, removes one nesting level |
| Typical use | change each element | expand and merge nested data |
| C# equivalent | Select | SelectMany |

The single-sentence rule: use `map` when each element maps to one element, and `flatMap` when each element maps to many and you want them merged.

### How do map and flatMap look side by side?

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;
import java.util.stream.Stream;

public class SideBySide {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Department> departments = List.of(
                new Department("Engineering", List.of(
                        new Employee("Rajesh", new BigDecimal("120000")))),
                new Department("HR", List.of(
                        new Employee("Priya", new BigDecimal("95000"))))
        );

        // map: one department -> one department name (one-to-one)
        List<String> names = departments.stream()
                .map(Department::getName)
                .collect(Collectors.toList());

        // flatMap: one department -> many employees (one-to-many, merged)
        List<Employee> employees = departments.stream()
                .flatMap(d -> d.getEmployees().stream())
                .collect(Collectors.toList());

        System.out.println("map result:     " + names);
        System.out.println("flatMap result: " + employees);
    }
}
```

```text
map result:     [Engineering, HR]
flatMap result: [Rajesh, Priya]
```

### What are common mistakes with flatMap?

The most common mistake is passing a mapper that returns a collection or a value instead of a stream. `flatMap` requires the mapper to return a `Stream`.

```java
// WRONG: mapper returns a List, not a Stream, so this does not compile
List<Employee> flat = departments.stream()
        .flatMap(d -> d.getEmployees())   // returns List<Employee>, not Stream
        .collect(Collectors.toList());
```

```java
// CORRECT: call .stream() so the mapper returns Stream<Employee>
List<Employee> flat = departments.stream()
        .flatMap(d -> d.getEmployees().stream())
        .collect(Collectors.toList());
```

Another mistake is forgetting to guard against a department whose employee list could be null.

### What are the edge cases?

If a department has an empty employee list, `flatMap` contributes nothing for it, which is correct and needs no special handling. But a null inner list throws a `NullPointerException` when you call `.stream()` on it.

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;
import java.util.stream.Stream;

public class EdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Department empty = new Department("Compliance", List.of());
        Department staffed = new Department("HR", List.of(
                new Employee("Priya", new BigDecimal("95000"))));

        List<Employee> flat = Stream.of(empty, staffed)
                .flatMap(d -> d.getEmployees() == null
                        ? Stream.empty()
                        : d.getEmployees().stream())
                .collect(Collectors.toList());

        System.out.println(flat); // empty department contributes nothing
    }
}
```

```text
[Priya]
```

Returning `Stream.empty()` for a null list keeps the pipeline safe.

### How does flatMap work with the primitive stream variants?

There are `flatMapToInt`, `flatMapToLong`, and `flatMapToDouble` for when the inner stream is a primitive stream, which avoids boxing. For example, summing all salaries by flattening into a stream of amounts.

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

public class SumSalaries {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Department> departments = List.of(
                new Department("Engineering", List.of(
                        new Employee("Rajesh", new BigDecimal("120000")),
                        new Employee("Anita", new BigDecimal("115000")))),
                new Department("HR", List.of(
                        new Employee("Priya", new BigDecimal("95000"))))
        );

        BigDecimal total = departments.stream()
                .flatMap(d -> d.getEmployees().stream())
                .map(Employee::getSalary)
                .reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add);

        System.out.println("total payroll = " + total);
    }
}
```

```text
total payroll = 330000
```

### How do you flatten more than two levels?

You can chain `flatMap` to flatten deeper structures, for example banks that hold departments that hold employees.

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class DeepFlatten {
    record Bank(String name, List<Department> departments) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Each flatMap peels one level of nesting.
        List<Bank> banks = List.of();
        List<Employee> everyone = banks.stream()
                .flatMap(b -> b.departments().stream())
                .flatMap(d -> d.getEmployees().stream())
                .collect(Collectors.toList());

        System.out.println("count = " + everyone.size());
    }
}
```

```text
count = 0
```

Each `flatMap` in the chain removes exactly one level of nesting.

### When should you use flatMap?

Use `flatMap` whenever one element naturally expands into many and you want them merged into a single stream: flattening nested lists, splitting a sentence into words, expanding an order into its line items, or turning a customer into their list of accounts. If each element maps to exactly one result, use `map` instead.

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class SplitWords {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> lines = List.of("transfer to Priya", "loan for Rajesh");

        List<String> words = lines.stream()
                .flatMap(line -> Arrays.stream(line.split(" ")))
                .collect(Collectors.toList());

        System.out.println(words);
    }
}
```

```text
[transfer, to, Priya, loan, for, Rajesh]
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Interviewers ask you to explain why the mapper must return a `Stream`, to contrast `flatMap` with `map` using the `Stream<Stream<T>>` result, to name the C# equivalent `SelectMany`, and to show `Optional.flatMap` which flattens nested Optionals the same way.

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.util.Optional;

public class OptionalFlatMap {
    static Optional<String> managerOf(String dept) {
        return dept.equals("HR") ? Optional.of("Priya") : Optional.empty();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Optional<String> dept = Optional.of("HR");

        // map would give Optional<Optional<String>>; flatMap keeps it flat
        Optional<String> manager = dept.flatMap(OptionalFlatMap::managerOf);

        System.out.println(manager.orElse("none"));
    }
}
```

```text
Priya
```
